For the polynomial graphed below, what is the nature of the zero at $x = 2$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-3,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.8:3.2,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.25*(\x+1)*(\x-2)*(\x-2)});
\filldraw (-1,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$-1$};
\filldraw (2,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graph below is of a polynomial of degree $4$ with another zero at $x = 1$. Which feature, together with this degree constraint, indicates that $x = -2$ is a zero of multiplicity $3$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-4,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-3) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-3.2:1.35,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.2*(\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x+2)*(1-\x)});
\filldraw (-2,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$-2$};
\filldraw (1,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
